1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,915
Complement deficiencies and hereditary

2
00:00:02,965 --> 00:00:06,659
angioedema come up in almost every allergy exam.

3
00:00:06,709 --> 00:00:09,936
One simple rule explains the deficiencies.

4
00:00:09,986 --> 00:00:14,149
Then we look closely at hereditary angioedema, or HAE.

5
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,327
Complement has three jobs.

6
00:00:16,377 --> 00:00:20,515
It clears immune complexes, it tags bacteria with C3b, and

7
00:00:20,565 --> 00:00:24,558
it lyses some bacteria with the membrane attack complex.

8
00:00:24,608 --> 00:00:28,962
Lose one job, and the disease tells you which job is missing.

9
00:00:29,333 --> 00:00:34,613
Think of it like a factory with three gates: waste disposal, tagging and a shredder.

10
00:00:34,663 --> 00:00:37,341
Break waste disposal and the factory clogs.

11
00:00:37,391 --> 00:00:41,021
Break only the shredder, and thin-walled packages survive.

12
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:44,821
Start with the early classical pathway.

13
00:00:44,871 --> 00:00:50,516
Genetic deficiencies of C1q, C1r, C4, C2 and C3 occur in humans.

14
00:00:50,566 --> 00:00:55,944
C2 deficiency is the most common human complement deficiency.

15
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:01,546
More than half of patients with C1q, C2 or C4 deficiency

16
00:01:01,596 --> 00:01:05,802
develop systemic lupus erythematosus, or SLE.

17
00:01:05,852 --> 00:01:09,869
Some patients with C2 or C4 deficiency have

18
00:01:09,919 --> 00:01:13,746
more infections; others have no symptoms.

19
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:14,794
Why lupus?

20
00:01:14,844 --> 00:01:19,256
These components clear immune complexes and apoptotic cells.

21
00:01:19,306 --> 00:01:24,239
Uncleared complexes deposit in vessel walls and cause inflammation.

22
00:01:24,289 --> 00:01:30,188
Uncleared apoptotic bodies are a likely source of the nuclear antigens in lupus.

23
00:01:30,567 --> 00:01:31,057
Now C3.

24
00:01:31,107 --> 00:01:35,537
C3 deficiency causes frequent, serious pyogenic infections

25
00:01:35,587 --> 00:01:40,249
that can be fatal, for example with Streptococcus pneumoniae.

26
00:01:40,299 --> 00:01:45,424
This shows the central role of C3 in opsonization and phagocytosis.

27
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:48,800
The lectin pathway gives a milder picture.

28
00:01:48,850 --> 00:01:53,810
Janeway says MBL deficiency is relatively common, about 5% of people.

29
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:58,022
It may cause more bacterial infections in early childhood.

30
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:00,521
Next, the alternative pathway.

31
00:02:00,571 --> 00:02:04,645
Its defects increase the risk of meningococcal infection.

32
00:02:04,695 --> 00:02:07,829
Factor B and factor D deficiencies are rare.

33
00:02:07,879 --> 00:02:11,809
X-linked properdin deficiency is more common, and these

34
00:02:11,859 --> 00:02:16,295
patients are especially susceptible to Neisseria meningitidis.

35
00:02:16,667 --> 00:02:21,791
The terminal components, C5 to C9, build the membrane attack complex.

36
00:02:21,841 --> 00:02:24,791
In their deficiency, the only consistent

37
00:02:24,841 --> 00:02:28,090
problem is disseminated Neisseria infection.

38
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:32,140
Lysis is important against these thin-walled bacteria.

39
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:33,783
How big is the risk?

40
00:02:33,833 --> 00:02:38,383
In a Japanese study, the yearly meningococcal risk was about 1 in 200

41
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:42,783
with a membrane attack deficiency, versus 1 in 2 million normally.

42
00:02:43,133 --> 00:02:44,264
Now the brakes.

43
00:02:44,314 --> 00:02:47,648
Without factor I, fluid-phase C3 convertase

44
00:02:47,698 --> 00:02:51,111
forms without control and uses up plasma C3.

45
00:02:51,161 --> 00:02:56,620
The result is a de facto C3 deficiency, with more pyogenic infections.

46
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,331
Factor H brakes the alternative pathway only.

47
00:03:00,381 --> 00:03:06,268
Its deficiency causes excess activation, C3 consumption and glomerulonephritis.

48
00:03:06,318 --> 00:03:09,649
The autoantibody C3 nephritic factor does the

49
00:03:09,699 --> 00:03:13,181
same: it protects the convertase from factor H.

50
00:03:13,533 --> 00:03:17,768
Defects in factor H, MCP or factor I can cause

51
00:03:17,818 --> 00:03:22,052
atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome, or aHUS.

52
00:03:22,102 --> 00:03:25,219
Heterozygous mutations are enough.

53
00:03:25,269 --> 00:03:32,670
Children present with haemolytic anaemia, low platelets and acute renal failure.

54
00:03:33,033 --> 00:03:38,506
Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria, or PNH, is different.

55
00:03:38,556 --> 00:03:41,407
CD59 and DAF need a GPI anchor.

56
00:03:41,457 --> 00:03:44,590
PIG-A mutations break this anchor.

57
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:51,984
Without CD59, the membrane attack complex forms on the patient's own red cells.

58
00:03:52,367 --> 00:03:59,089
The result is intravascular haemolysis, chronic anaemia and venous thrombosis.

59
00:03:59,139 --> 00:04:03,082
Exam point: the PNH mutation is not inherited.

60
00:04:03,132 --> 00:04:07,336
It is an acquired somatic mutation in stem cells.

61
00:04:07,386 --> 00:04:11,504
Abbas notes anti-C5 antibodies for PNH and aHUS.

62
00:04:11,867 --> 00:04:17,029
Now the most important brake for allergists: C1 inhibitor.

63
00:04:17,079 --> 00:04:18,377
It is a serpin.

64
00:04:18,427 --> 00:04:22,331
It is the major inhibitor of C1r, C1s, MASP1

65
00:04:22,381 --> 00:04:26,376
and MASP2, plasma kallikrein and factor XIIa.

66
00:04:26,733 --> 00:04:33,812
HAE type 1 and 2 is a rare autosomal dominant disease, in about 1 in 50,000 people.

67
00:04:33,862 --> 00:04:37,419
More than 450 SERPING1 mutations cause it.

68
00:04:37,469 --> 00:04:41,370
In 20 to 25% of patients, the mutation is new.

69
00:04:41,733 --> 00:04:43,738
Here is the swelling pathway.

70
00:04:43,788 --> 00:04:47,705
Factor XII autoactivates on negatively charged surfaces.

71
00:04:47,755 --> 00:04:51,672
Factor XIIa converts prekallikrein to plasma kallikrein.

72
00:04:51,722 --> 00:04:56,985
Kallikrein cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen and releases bradykinin.

73
00:04:57,367 --> 00:05:01,664
Bradykinin binds the B2 receptor and makes vessels leaky.

74
00:05:01,714 --> 00:05:04,333
ACE quickly breaks bradykinin down.

75
00:05:04,383 --> 00:05:08,604
C1 inhibitor normally brakes factor XIIa and kallikrein.

76
00:05:08,654 --> 00:05:11,121
Without it, bradykinin builds up.

77
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:16,250
So bradykinin is the primary mediator of swelling in HAE.

78
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:19,871
The missing brake also lets complement run.

79
00:05:19,921 --> 00:05:23,408
Excess C4 and C2 are cleaved, so C4 falls.

80
00:05:23,767 --> 00:05:25,661
This explains a key clue.

81
00:05:25,711 --> 00:05:28,850
Mast cell angioedema is histamine-driven.

82
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:33,284
HAE swelling is bradykinin-driven, so it does not respond

83
00:05:33,334 --> 00:05:37,173
to antihistamines, glucocorticoids or epinephrine.

84
00:05:37,533 --> 00:05:43,862
Suspect HAE in recurrent angioedema with family history, onset in childhood,

85
00:05:43,912 --> 00:05:49,569
painful abdominal attacks, airway swelling, prodromes and no wheals.

86
00:05:49,619 --> 00:05:52,170
Median onset is about 12 years.

87
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:55,695
Family history may be absent in up to 25%.

88
00:05:56,067 --> 00:05:59,242
Triggers include dental or surgical trauma,

89
00:05:59,292 --> 00:06:02,842
oestrogens, stress, fatigue and febrile illness.

90
00:06:02,892 --> 00:06:07,267
ACE inhibitors can trigger attacks, so avoid them strictly.

91
00:06:07,633 --> 00:06:09,150
Now the lab work-up.

92
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:15,730
Test every suspected patient for C1 inhibitor function, C1 inhibitor protein and C4.

93
00:06:15,780 --> 00:06:17,454
Repeat any low result.

94
00:06:17,504 --> 00:06:21,527
C3 and CH50 are usually normal, so they do not help.

95
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:23,298
Read the pattern.

96
00:06:23,348 --> 00:06:28,920
HAE type 1 is about 85% of patients: low protein and low function.

97
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:33,690
HAE type 2 has normal or high protein, but low function.

98
00:06:33,740 --> 00:06:36,757
C4 is usually low, but not specific.

99
00:06:37,133 --> 00:06:44,426
Acquired C1 inhibitor deficiency has the same basic profile, but C1q is low in 75%.

100
00:06:44,476 --> 00:06:50,352
Think of it with new angioedema after age 40 and no family history.

101
00:06:50,402 --> 00:06:52,652
Look for lymphoma or MGUS.

102
00:06:53,033 --> 00:06:59,029
HAE with normal C1 inhibitor is very rare and looks like type 1 and 2.

103
00:06:59,079 --> 00:07:05,420
Some patients have a factor XII mutation, but most have no known mutation.

104
00:07:05,470 --> 00:07:10,084
ACE inhibitor angioedema also has normal C1 inhibitor.

105
00:07:10,467 --> 00:07:12,958
Now treatment, with a warning.

106
00:07:13,008 --> 00:07:20,073
Everything here follows the 2017 WAO and EAACI guideline, for HAE type 1 and 2 only.

107
00:07:20,123 --> 00:07:26,427
Drugs and licences change, so check the current guideline before your exam.

108
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:30,554
Per the 2017 guideline, treat attacks as early

109
00:07:30,604 --> 00:07:34,275
as possible, and always treat airway attacks.

110
00:07:34,325 --> 00:07:37,830
Use C1 inhibitor, ecallantide or icatibant.

111
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:42,047
If none is available, use solvent detergent-treated

112
00:07:42,097 --> 00:07:44,776
plasma, then fresh frozen plasma.

113
00:07:45,133 --> 00:07:48,313
Each drug hits the cascade at a different point.

114
00:07:48,363 --> 00:07:51,945
C1 inhibitor concentrate replaces the missing protein.

115
00:07:51,995 --> 00:07:54,569
Ecallantide inhibits plasma kallikrein.

116
00:07:54,619 --> 00:07:57,529
Icatibant blocks the bradykinin B2 receptor.

117
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:01,343
Tranexamic acid and androgens do not work for attacks.

118
00:08:01,393 --> 00:08:05,095
In progressive airway swelling, consider intubation early.

119
00:08:05,145 --> 00:08:08,264
Every patient carries medication for two attacks.

120
00:08:08,633 --> 00:08:14,198
For prevention, the 2017 guideline gives C1 inhibitor before procedures.

121
00:08:14,248 --> 00:08:20,748
For long-term prophylaxis, C1 inhibitor is first line and androgens are second line.

122
00:08:20,798 --> 00:08:23,711
Antifibrinolytics are not recommended.

123
00:08:24,067 --> 00:08:27,715
In children under 12, use C1 inhibitor for attacks,

124
00:08:27,765 --> 00:08:30,978
and avoid androgens before Tanner stage five.

125
00:08:31,028 --> 00:08:36,416
In pregnancy, C1 inhibitor is preferred, and androgens are contraindicated.

126
00:08:36,767 --> 00:08:38,981
Five things to remember.

127
00:08:39,031 --> 00:08:44,076
One: early classical defects cause lupus-like disease.

128
00:08:44,126 --> 00:08:48,983
Two: C3 or factor I loss causes pyogenic infections.

129
00:08:49,033 --> 00:08:54,927
Three: C5 to C9 and properdin loss causes Neisseria infections.

130
00:08:54,977 --> 00:08:58,607
Four: HAE is bradykinin, not histamine.

131
00:08:58,657 --> 00:09:03,231
Five: test C1 inhibitor function, protein and C4.

132
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:04,916
And one exam trap.

133
00:09:04,966 --> 00:09:10,681
Normal C1 inhibitor level and function do not exclude hereditary angioedema.

134
00:09:10,731 --> 00:09:15,309
With a family history, think of HAE with normal C1 inhibitor.

135
00:09:15,359 --> 00:09:17,888
Next: the ten-minute rapid review.
